<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
        The schema half of the shift from reactive to continuous collection.

        Before this changeset, an EvidenceItem existed because an alert fired. After it,
        evidence streams in from registered sources on its own schedule and an alert is
        something that happens *alongside* it. Two things have to exist in the schema
        before that is possible:

          1. A registered source (evidence_source). Under the reactive model the producer
             was anonymous — anything could publish to the topic and the alert carried the
             classification. Under continuous collection the producer is a long-lived,
             named participant whose silence is itself a finding, and whose registration
             is where classification is decided.

          2. A coverage window on each item (event_start / event_end). Correlation asks
             "what evidence covers the time this alert fired", and nothing in the schema
             could answer that: evidence_item.created_at is when *we preserved* the
             artifact, which for a streamed log bundle is minutes-to-hours after the
             activity it records.

        0005 adds the correlation itself.
    -->

    <changeSet id="0004-1" author="dfraas-team">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <tableExists tableName="evidence_source"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Creates evidence_source — the registered producer that streams evidence in continuously</comment>
        <createTable tableName="evidence_source">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>

            <!--
                The stable identifier a producer puts on the wire, and the same value
                trigger_event.source_system carries. Deliberately the natural key rather
                than the UUID: a collection agent's config file has to name its source, and
                a human-readable key is what survives being copied into one.
            -->
            <column name="source_key" type="VARCHAR(255)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>

            <column name="display_name" type="VARCHAR(255)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>

            <!--
                Registration is where classification is decided — this is the answer to
                README open question 1.

                Under the reactive model, classification had to be inferred per alert, from
                a SIEM alert type, by a mapping table nobody had written. Under continuous
                collection the question moves to a much better place: a source is registered
                once, by a human, who knows which business unit the system it collects from
                belongs to. Every item that source produces inherits that classification.

                NOT NULL for the same reason evidence_item.work_group_id is: a source whose
                evidence cannot be classified has no defensible retention or release policy,
                and registering it would be recording a promise the system cannot keep.
            -->
            <column name="work_group_id" type="UUID">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>

            <!--
                What kind of producer this is (SYSLOG, DB_AUDIT, NETFLOW, EDR, ...).
                VARCHAR and not a CHECK list, for the same reason trigger_type is free-form:
                the set is defined by whatever the institution actually runs, and adding a
                collector must not require a migration.
            -->
            <column name="source_type" type="VARCHAR(64)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>

            <!-- VARCHAR + CHECK (0004-4), matching evidence_item.status. -->
            <column name="status" type="VARCHAR(32)" defaultValue="ACTIVE">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>

            <!--
                How often this source is expected to produce, in seconds. NULLABLE, and the
                null means something specific: "this source is event-driven and silence
                proves nothing". A syslog forwarder should have a cadence; a source that
                only emits on a rare condition should not, because holding it to one would
                generate a permanent false alarm.

                This column is the whole reason evidence_source exists as an entity rather
                than a lookup string. Continuous collection has a failure mode the reactive
                model did not: a source that silently stops looks exactly like a source with
                nothing to report. Under the old design a missing alert was noticeable —
                someone was waiting for it. Here, nobody is. Cadence plus last_evidence_at
                is what makes "which of our sources has gone quiet" a query rather than a
                hope, and that question is the readiness audit.
            -->
            <column name="expected_interval_seconds" type="INTEGER">
                <constraints nullable="true"/>
            </column>

            <!--
                Last time this source successfully produced an item. The one mutable column
                on the row, and stamped by the database clock on each ingest.

                Note this is a liveness signal, not evidence: it is overwritten, so it keeps
                no history. The history of what a source produced is evidence_item itself.
            -->
            <column name="last_evidence_at" type="TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE">
                <constraints nullable="true"/>
            </column>

            <column name="registered_at" type="TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
        <rollback>
            <dropTable tableName="evidence_source"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-2" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>A source key is the natural key producers put on the wire, so it must be unique</comment>
        <!--
            Global, not composite. Unlike trigger_event.external_event_id — which is only
            unique within the system that issued it, because we do not control SIEM alert
            numbering — source_key is assigned by *us* at registration, so we can and should
            require it to be unique outright.
        -->
        <addUniqueConstraint
            tableName="evidence_source"
            columnNames="source_key"
            constraintName="uq_evidence_source_key"/>
        <rollback>
            <dropUniqueConstraint
                tableName="evidence_source"
                constraintName="uq_evidence_source_key"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-3" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>Foreign key to work_group — RESTRICT, as everywhere else in this schema</comment>
        <!--
            RESTRICT rather than CASCADE: deleting a classification must not silently
            deregister the collectors filing under it, which would stop evidence arriving
            with no error anywhere. A source that should stop is set DECOMMISSIONED, not
            deleted — the same soft-delete instinct evidence_item.status encodes.
        -->
        <addForeignKeyConstraint
            constraintName="fk_evidence_source_work_group"
            baseTableName="evidence_source"
            baseColumnNames="work_group_id"
            referencedTableName="work_group"
            referencedColumnNames="id"
            onDelete="RESTRICT"
            onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
        <rollback>
            <dropForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="evidence_source" constraintName="fk_evidence_source_work_group"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-4" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>Constrain source status to the known states, and cadence to a real interval</comment>
        <!-- addCheckConstraint is a Liquibase Pro tag, so these are raw SQL — same
             trade-off as ck_evidence_item_status, and taken for the same reason. -->
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE evidence_source
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_source_status
                CHECK (status IN ('ACTIVE', 'SUSPENDED', 'DECOMMISSIONED'));
        </sql>
        <!--
            A zero or negative cadence would make the silence check fire on every pass,
            which is worse than having no check at all: an alarm that is always on is an
            alarm nobody reads.
        -->
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE evidence_source
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_source_expected_interval
                CHECK (expected_interval_seconds IS NULL OR expected_interval_seconds &gt; 0);
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE evidence_source DROP CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_source_expected_interval;</sql>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE evidence_source DROP CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_source_status;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-5" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>Index the work_group foreign key and the liveness-monitoring predicate</comment>
        <createIndex tableName="evidence_source" indexName="ix_evidence_source_work_group_id">
            <column name="work_group_id"/>
        </createIndex>
        <!--
            Raw SQL for the partial index (no WHERE support in createIndex outside
            Liquibase Pro).

            Partial on exactly the rows the liveness sweep looks at: sources that are
            supposed to be producing. A source with no cadence expectation, or one that is
            suspended or decommissioned, cannot be "late" and only dilutes the index.
        -->
        <sql>
            CREATE INDEX ix_evidence_source_liveness
                ON evidence_source (last_evidence_at)
                WHERE status = 'ACTIVE' AND expected_interval_seconds IS NOT NULL;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX ix_evidence_source_liveness;</sql>
            <dropIndex tableName="evidence_source" indexName="ix_evidence_source_work_group_id"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-6" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>evidence_item gains its producing source, that source's own artifact id, and a coverage window</comment>
        <!--
            All four columns are nullable, and each null means something different:

            - evidence_source_id NULL: preserved by a person, not streamed. Manual
              preservation and compliance holds have no collector behind them, and
              inventing a "manual" source row would assert that a collector exists.
              Provenance is not lost — that is CustodyEvent.performed_by's job.

            - source_artifact_id NULL: the producer had no identifier of its own to give.

            - event_start / event_end NULL: the coverage window is unknown. Such an item is
              still fully preserved evidence; it simply cannot be *automatically* correlated
              and can only be linked to a trigger event by hand. Making these NOT NULL was
              rejected: it would either reject legitimate evidence at ingest, or force a
              backfill that invents a coverage window from created_at — asserting when an
              artifact's contents were produced on the basis of when we happened to store
              it, which is exactly the kind of fabricated timestamp this schema goes out of
              its way to avoid elsewhere.
        -->
        <addColumn tableName="evidence_item">
            <column name="evidence_source_id" type="UUID">
                <constraints nullable="true"/>
            </column>

            <!--
                The producer's own identifier for this artifact, and the dedupe key for
                streamed ingestion — the evidence-side equivalent of
                trigger_event.external_event_id.

                This matters more under continuous collection than it did before. Kafka is
                at-least-once, and the volume here is orders of magnitude above the alert
                stream, so redelivery is not an edge case but a daily occurrence. Until now
                the only thing standing against a duplicate item was
                uq_evidence_item_storage_ref, which only helps if the storage key happens to
                be derived deterministically from the artifact — a property nothing enforced
                and no comment claimed.
            -->
            <column name="source_artifact_id" type="VARCHAR(255)">
                <constraints nullable="true"/>
            </column>

            <!--
                The interval of activity this artifact actually covers, as asserted by the
                source. This is the axis correlation runs along, and it is emphatically not
                created_at: an hourly log bundle rolled at 15:00 and preserved at 15:02
                covers 14:00-15:00, and correlating an alert that fired at 14:30 against
                created_at would miss it entirely while matching bundles that contain
                nothing relevant.

                Sender-asserted, and treated with the same suspicion as
                trigger_event.fired_at — kept alongside created_at (our clock) rather than
                replacing it. A source with a skewed clock can misplace its own evidence in
                time; the gap between the window and created_at is what makes that visible.

                A point-in-time artifact (a memory dump, a single transaction record) sets
                both to the same instant. That is why the correlation range is built with
                inclusive '[]' bounds in 0004-9 — a half-open range over a zero-length
                interval is empty, and would silently overlap nothing at all.
            -->
            <column name="event_start" type="TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE">
                <constraints nullable="true"/>
            </column>
            <column name="event_end" type="TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE">
                <constraints nullable="true"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
        <rollback>
            <dropColumn tableName="evidence_item" columnName="event_end"/>
            <dropColumn tableName="evidence_item" columnName="event_start"/>
            <dropColumn tableName="evidence_item" columnName="source_artifact_id"/>
            <dropColumn tableName="evidence_item" columnName="evidence_source_id"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-7" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>Foreign key and dedupe key for the producing source</comment>
        <!--
            RESTRICT: a source that produced evidence cannot be deleted out from under it.
            The evidence would survive, but the record of what collected it would not, and
            "where did this come from" is the first question asked of any exhibit.

            Note what is NOT being done here: work_group_id stays on evidence_item and is
            not replaced by a join through evidence_source. It looks redundant — a source
            declares a work group, so the item's classification could be derived. It is not
            redundant, it is a snapshot. Reclassifying a source next year must not silently
            reclassify every artifact it ever produced, each of which was retained and
            released under the classification in force at the time.
        -->
        <addForeignKeyConstraint
            constraintName="fk_evidence_item_evidence_source"
            baseTableName="evidence_item"
            baseColumnNames="evidence_source_id"
            referencedTableName="evidence_source"
            referencedColumnNames="id"
            onDelete="RESTRICT"
            onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
        <!--
            The streamed-ingestion idempotency guarantee. Composite for the same reason
            uq_trigger_event_source_external is: an artifact id is only meaningful within
            the source that issued it.

            Postgres treats NULLs as distinct in a unique constraint by default, which is
            precisely the behaviour wanted here: manually preserved items carry NULL in both
            columns and never collide with each other, while a streamed source that
            redelivers artifact "auth-2026-09-05-14" is rejected on the second attempt.
        -->
        <addUniqueConstraint
            tableName="evidence_item"
            columnNames="evidence_source_id, source_artifact_id"
            constraintName="uq_evidence_item_source_artifact"/>
        <rollback>
            <dropUniqueConstraint tableName="evidence_item" constraintName="uq_evidence_item_source_artifact"/>
            <dropForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="evidence_item" constraintName="fk_evidence_item_evidence_source"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-8" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>Coverage window must be whole and ordered; an artifact id needs a source to be meaningful</comment>
        <!--
            Half a window is worse than none: it looks answerable to a correlation query
            and answers wrongly. Either both ends are known or neither is.
        -->
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE evidence_item
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_item_coverage_window
                CHECK (
                    (event_start IS NULL AND event_end IS NULL)
                    OR (event_start IS NOT NULL AND event_end IS NOT NULL AND event_end &gt;= event_start)
                );
        </sql>
        <!--
            An artifact id without a source cannot be resolved, and worse, it would sit
            outside uq_evidence_item_source_artifact (NULLs being distinct) and so dedupe
            against nothing at all.
        -->
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE evidence_item
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_item_artifact_needs_source
                CHECK (source_artifact_id IS NULL OR evidence_source_id IS NOT NULL);
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE evidence_item DROP CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_item_artifact_needs_source;</sql>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE evidence_item DROP CONSTRAINT ck_evidence_item_coverage_window;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="0004-9" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>Index the source foreign key, and the coverage window as a GiST range index</comment>
        <createIndex tableName="evidence_item" indexName="ix_evidence_item_evidence_source_id">
            <column name="evidence_source_id"/>
        </createIndex>
        <!--
            The index correlation actually runs on, and the reason the columns are stored as
            two scalars rather than a native range type.

            A btree on (event_start, event_end) cannot serve an overlap predicate well: the
            "does this interval intersect that one" test is not a prefix range scan, and on
            a table holding every artifact a bank's estate produces it degrades to something
            close to a sequential scan. GiST over a range expression supports the &amp;&amp;
            operator directly, which is the difference between correlation being a query and
            being a batch job.

            Two scalar columns rather than a tstzrange column, with the range built in the
            index expression: Hibernate has no native mapping for tstzrange, and with
            ddl-auto=validate a column it cannot map is a startup problem rather than a
            convenience. The scalars stay the source of truth and are what the entity maps;
            the range exists only where it is useful, which is here.

            Inclusive '[]' bounds, matching 0004-6: a point-in-time artifact has
            event_start = event_end, and tstzrange(t, t, '[)') is the empty range, which
            overlaps nothing. With '[]' it is the single instant t, which is the truth.

            Partial, because an item with no coverage window has no place in a correlation
            index: tstzrange(NULL, NULL, '[]') is the *unbounded* range, so without the
            WHERE clause every unwindowed item would overlap every alert ever fired.
        -->
        <sql>
            CREATE INDEX ix_evidence_item_coverage
                ON evidence_item
                USING gist (tstzrange(event_start, event_end, '[]'))
                WHERE event_start IS NOT NULL;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX ix_evidence_item_coverage;</sql>
            <dropIndex tableName="evidence_item" indexName="ix_evidence_item_evidence_source_id"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
